Z||000000||drednot||05-12-2001||02:03 PM||||How much should a clutch job cost on a 1985 Pontic Fiero 4 cyl 5 speed?||||reg||4
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||05-12-2001||02:09 PM||||I Have done my own Clutch replacement. but if I remember coorectly to have it done by a independent shop here in Phx.Az. I was quoted about $500.00 parts and labor.after donig the job my self I thought It might have been worth the money to have it done.hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000002||LZeitgeist||05-12-2001||03:04 PM||||In central NC, between $800 - $1000.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||05-12-2001||03:24 PM||||$900, but i went to a pretty reputable shop. i read where you can do it through the wheelwell. i may try that next time.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierohoho||05-12-2001||05:22 PM||||I say shop around to different transmission shops, prices vary but not much, you might get lucky.<P>Also, if you are in a Fiero club the club may have a shop that will give you a discount.<P>Last thought, are you sure it needs a clutch, or could your hydraulic system need bleeding? <P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||05-12-2001||05:27 PM||||Yea, about $1000 around the NC area.  I did it myself (with LZeitgeist's help - thanks guy  <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/otn/party/beerchug.gif">  ).  I spent about $300 in parts (clutch, input shaft bearing, machine shop labor to press in bearing).  Much better than $1000.  It's not an impossible job for the average home mechanic, if you have a buddy and a good work area.  We did mine without any special tools or power tools, save for a 4 foot breaker bar for a couple of stubborn cradle bolts.  We took mine whole tranny out through the wheel well.  This is definately the way to go for the home mechanic.||||reg||4
Z||000006||drednot||05-12-2001||07:06 PM||||hold on a second... How could a clutch job cost $1000 !?!?  If that is the case, the car is way totaled!  A clutch for my Plymouth cost $425 installed!  I just don't see how a car could be that badly designed that it would cost $1000 for a fricking clutch!  If I can't find a shop to replace this thing for less than $600 I am un-loading this piece of crap at the closest ditch, creek or scrapyard.  Can you tell I’m pissed?  Man I wish I had an automatic!  I will shop around a little more, If I can't find anyone that will do it for $600 or less, that will be it for this car.||||reg||8
Z||000007||FieroLisa||05-12-2001||07:08 PM||||I had the clutch done on my coupe twice(I learned to drive stick on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )... Each time it cost $200, but I don't know all that was done. I know thats cheap for a clutch, so I don't know if it was just the cable or what- once it was done by AATCO and once by a local mechanic.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick's Dad||05-12-2001||07:41 PM||||Lisa, you probably just smiled and the guys waived the labor charge.... This is how I "lost" an old printer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>My 2m4 and Formula were both about $900, with one $1200 job that included the forks, bearing, and assorted other stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000009||sjp777||05-12-2001||08:01 PM||||I had three replaced on my 86. Always cost between $450 and $600. If you someone is trying to charge you $1000, hand them a jar of vaseline and bend over.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||05-12-2001||08:10 PM||||If you can't afford it, why are you driving it. <P>I really need a bumper that says that. So many people see a car as a free ride/right. Either pay to have it done, learn to do it yourself, or quit driving it. Most places still have public transit.||||reg||1
Z||000011||PBJ||05-12-2001||08:22 PM||||A garage with a hoist and air tools-should only been a 3-4 hour job.  It took me 4 hours to do my first and 2.5 hr to do my second on my next car.  I would say $600.00 should cover a 4 cyl clutch job.  Just have to find a place that is not AFRAID of a fiero||||reg||1
Z||000012||sjp777||05-12-2001||09:48 PM||||Buy the heavy duty clutch from Fiero Store for $220 and take it to a shop to have it installed. Most shops work off a standard price grid for determining labor costs for each job. I checked a couple shops and just labor with no parts is around $300 to $350. For less than $600 total you should have a new clutch that is better than stock<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Brucepts||05-12-2001||10:54 PM||||You can "ditch" it at my house and I will take it to the scrap yard for you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .||||reg||1
Z||000014||Indiana_resto_guy||05-13-2001||06:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>If I can't find a shop to replace this thing for less than $600 I am un-loading this piece of crap at the closest ditch, creek or scrapyard. <BR> If I can't find anyone that will do it for $600 or less, that will be it for this car.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Find a shop that is not afraid of a Fiero. A $1,000.00 quote indicates they don't want to do it!<BR>Anyone with a brain could figure this out! <BR>I hope I wasn't too blunt for you I read you first post and considered it unworthy for a reply because of this point. (In response to yur topic header)<BR>Compare with a dealer price in this area and go from there, they want $800.00.<P>It should cost no more than anyother FWD manual trans clutch, if the shop knows what they're doing it should take 4 hours max.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||thomas_l||05-13-2001||09:47 AM||||My Chilton labor guide gives 8.3 hours for the clutch and another 0.3 to renew the fork/bushings. SO, multiply 8.5 times the labor rate at your favorite shop. Any clutch parts you see in the books - add 30-50% markup. Don't forget the flywheel should be turned too. Buy it yourself and most shops won't gaurantee it. That being said, my guess is you will be seeing a total bill around $600-800. Unless more cobbled up stuff is found during the job and that would, of course, be extra (i.e. motor/trans mounts, exhaust mounts, clutch hydraulics, wheel bearings, brakes). Some shops will automatically add extras like output seals to make sure your fresh clutch job doesn't come back with related trouble. And then there's some rats out there that will just stick a disc in. Or do it yourself for about $200 in parts and maybe a few new tools.<P>Oh yeah (nomex on) most semi-skilled techs can beat 8.5 hours. Just remember that most repair shops are not charities and exist to make money so expect to pay a reasonable mark-up on parts and book labor. And remember if it breaks inside the gaurantee (you ARE getting one right? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) they do the next one free.<p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 05-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||fyerstarter||05-13-2001||11:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by drednot:<BR><B>hold on a second... How could a clutch job cost $1000 !?!?  If that is the case, the car is way totaled!  A clutch for my Plymouth cost $425 installed!  I just don't see how a car could be that badly designed that it would cost $1000 for a fricking clutch!  If I can't find a shop to replace this thing for less than $600 I am un-loading this piece of crap at the closest ditch, creek or scrapyard.  Can you tell I’m pissed?  Man I wish I had an automatic!  I will shop around a little more, If I can't find anyone that will do it for $600 or less, that will be it for this car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Well it is a mid-engine car which makes it not quite as easy to work on as a front engine car. But that doesn't necessarily mean that it was poorly designed. Consider other mid-engine cars such as Lamborgini, Lotus, Ferrari, Mr2. I would imagine the price for cluth work on those is quite high as well. Does that mean they are poorly designed?....well with the exception of the mR2 :P    j/k<P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Vader||05-13-2001||12:20 PM||||I've got it down to about a 5-hour job in the garage with only a floor jack and crane.  I can remove the trans in about 1½ hours, swap the pressure plate/clutch assembly, and get it back in in another couple of hours.  The PITA is getting the rear aligned after the work, refilling the trans, and all the other little nuisance tasks that go along with the project.  I've replaced five clutches this way, and get faster every time.  Then again, I don't follow the book procedure, either.<P><I>Here's an outline:</I><P>Remove the clutch slave cylinder, transmission shift cables, and VSS sensor from the transaxle.  Remove the ground strap at the lower end of the transmission.  Remove the clutch release arm from the clutch fork shaft.  Remove all transmission to engine bolts except for an upper and rear lower to hold the assembly together.  <P>Raise the rear of the vehicle and place it on stands at the rear space frame rails, near the extreme rear of the car.  Start draining the transaxle.  Remove the rear wheels and scribe/paint lines or marks on the upper strut mounting flanges.  Remove the rear calipers, rotors, lower ball joint lock bolts, and  upper strut mount bolts.  Separate the lower ball joints from the control arms and remove both half-axle assemblies with the knuckles attached from the transaxle case.  Remove the three upper strut mounting nuts on the left strut, and lower the strut out of the car.  Keeping the strut intact as an assembly eliminates the need for a spring compressor and the extra work.<P>So far, this is exactly the same as what you would do on the other end of most front drive GM cars.<P>Remove the plastic push fasteners and self-tapping screws from the splash shields and left inner fender liner, and remove the LR inner fender.  Don't forget the PITA screws into the rocker moldings.  Disconnect the park brake cable at the three-way connection under the rear/center cross member.  Remove the park brake cable from the rear cradle and move it out of the way in the left wheel opening.  <P>Loosen the front and rear transmission mount bolts to the point of removal, but keep them in for locating the engine in place.  <P>Loosen the rear engine/transaxle cradle to body attachment bolts.  If the car you have is a rust bucket, the weld nuts in the frame rails will probably break loose and spin free in the space frame - another PITA.   Support the rear of the cradle with a jack and completely remove the bolts.  Slowly lower the cradle, watching for interference from wiring and throttle and shifting cables.  Lower the cradle a few inches only to make sure everything will clear.  When the cradle is able to move freely without hanging / ripping the front (right) engine mount, stop lowering.  Attach a crane to the engine handling loop at the rear (left) end of the engine and raise the engine just slightly.  Remove the remaining transmission bolts to the engine and the transmission mount bolts.  The front transmission mount is no treat, either.  Remove the transmission from the engine.  It will have to sit loosely in the engine bay until you make room to remove it from the car.  <P>Support the engine with the crane, and lower the cradle as far as it will go.  The transmission will need to be manipulated and turned as it is taken out the left wheel opening.  I have had good success using a small scissors jack to spread the cradle from the body while removing the transmission.  There is just barely enough room to spin the trans out of there.  The shift cable anchor tabs will become a point of interference, but can be worked out past the lower edge of the steel wheel opening and the top of the engine cradle.  Are we having fun yet?<P>Once it's out, it's like any other 76mm transaxle.  Replacing the clutch fork is almost a necessity, since you don't want to have the old fork fail shortly after the clutch is replaced.  You probably don't need/want the rework anyway.<P>Fight the transaxle back in the same way, then reverse the procedure for assembly.  With having done this several times, and judiciously using Never-Seez on the problem fasteners, along with some air tools and low expectations, I've been able to do the whole thing in about five hours working straight and alone.  If you have some extra hands available when removing /installing the trans, the rest is fairly normal.<P>Good luck.  If I have to do this again, I'm going to photo-document the procedure to make it clearer.  I've tried to explain this to several people more than once, and some clear pictures would be a real help.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
